. Make a strand-aware gene BED file

Your Liftoff annotation already contains gene features:

gff="J702.liftoff.gff3"

awk -F'\t' '
BEGIN {OFS="\t"}
$0 !~ /^#/ && $3=="gene" && $1 ~ /^chr([1-9]|10)_RagTag$/ {
    id=$9
    sub(/^.*ID=/,"",id)
    sub(/;.*/,"",id)

    print $1,$4-1,$5,id,0,$7
}' "$gff" |
sort -k1,1 -k2,2n \
> J702.genes.bed

The six BED columns are:

chromosome  start  end  gene_ID  score  strand

The strand column is essential: deepTools will orient negative-strand genes so that every gene runs from TSS to TES in the plot.

2. Convert indels to one-base event positions

For a metagene event-density plot, represent each indel by its starting position rather than its deleted sequence length:

bcftools query \
    -f '%CHROM\t%POS0\t%POS\n' \
    private_deepvariant_indels.vcf.gz |
awk '$1 ~ /^chr([1-9]|10)_RagTag$/' |
sort -k1,1 -k2,2n \
> private_indels.positions.bed

This prevents a 100-bp deletion from contributing 100 times more signal than a one-base deletion. Each indel represents one mutation event.

3. Make chromosome sizes
samtools faidx J702_flip.fa

awk '$1 ~ /^chr([1-9]|10)_RagTag$/' \
    J702_flip.fa.fai |
cut -f1,2 \
> J702.chr1-10.sizes
4. Convert indel events to bedGraph and bigWig
bedtools genomecov \
    -bg \
    -i private_indels.positions.bed \
    -g J702.chr1-10.sizes \
> private_indels.bedGraph

Sort it:

LC_ALL=C sort -k1,1 -k2,2n \
    private_indels.bedGraph \
> private_indels.sorted.bedGraph

Convert to bigWig:

bedGraphToBigWig \
    private_indels.sorted.bedGraph \
    J702.chr1-10.sizes \
    private_indels.bw

If bedGraphToBigWig is unavailable, it can normally be installed with:

conda install -c bioconda ucsc-bedgraphtobigwig
5. Scale all genes to the same length

This example uses:

20 kb upstream
100 normalized gene-body bins
20 kb downstream
200-bp bins in the flanking regions
A scaled gene-body length of 20 kb
computeMatrix scale-regions \
    -S private_indels.bw \
    -R J702.genes.bed \
    --beforeRegionStartLength 20000 \
    --regionBodyLength 20000 \
    --afterRegionStartLength 20000 \
    --binSize 200 \
    --missingDataAsZero \
    --skipZeros \
    --numberOfProcessors 8 \
    -o private_indels.metagene.matrix.gz \
    --outFileNameMatrix private_indels.metagene.matrix.tsv

However, remove --skipZeros if you want the true average across all genes, including genes containing no indels:

computeMatrix scale-regions \
    -S private_indels.bw \
    -R J702.genes.bed \
    --beforeRegionStartLength 20000 \
    --regionBodyLength 20000 \
    --afterRegionStartLength 20000 \
    --binSize 200 \
    --missingDataAsZero \
    --numberOfProcessors 8 \
    -o private_indels.metagene.matrix.gz \
    --outFileNameMatrix private_indels.metagene.matrix.tsv

For your question, the second version is correct. Using --skipZeros would retain only genes near indels and inflate the average.

6. Plot the average indel profile
plotProfile \
    -m private_indels.metagene.matrix.gz \
    --plotType lines \
    --averageType mean \
    --startLabel TSS \
    --endLabel TES \
    --regionsLabel "All genes" \
    --samplesLabel "Private indels" \
    --yAxisLabel "Mean indel density" \
    --plotTitle "Distribution of indels across maize genes" \
    --plotFile private_indels.metagene_profile.pdf \
    --outFileNameData private_indels.metagene_profile.tsv

